# Project-curated cppcheck suppressions for pgxbackup.
#
# Each entry MUST have a comment immediately above explaining why the suppression is sound. Do not add suppressions to silence
# real findings; if a finding is real, fix it (or use an inline `// cppcheck-suppress <id>` annotation at the call site if a
# narrowly-scoped suppression is justified).
#
# Format: <check-id>[:<file>[:<line>]]
# Bare check-id suppresses globally. Path-scoped suppression is preferred; global suppression should only be used when the
# false-positive pattern is intrinsic to the codebase (e.g., a project-wide macro the checker cannot reason about).

# cppcheck cannot see across the FUNCTION_TEST_BEGIN/FUNCTION_LOG_BEGIN/FUNCTION_TEST_VOID family of macros (declared in
# src/common/debug.h). Each of those expands to a state-machine that declares a sentinel local FUNCTION_TEST_BEGIN_exists which
# is consumed by the matching FUNCTION_TEST_RETURN/FUNCTION_LOG_RETURN macro -- but cppcheck only sees the declaration, not the
# cross-macro consumption, and so reports `unassignedVariable` on the sentinel. The macro pattern frames hundreds of functions
# across the codebase, so the suppression is global rather than path-scoped.
unassignedVariable

# manifest.c records a memory-context size before a loop and consumes it after the loop, both inside `#ifdef DEBUG_MEM` blocks.
# cppcheck does not follow preprocessor branches and so reports `unreadVariable` on the assignment. The variable IS read in the
# matching #ifdef DEBUG_MEM block further down. Real consumption verified at src/info/manifest/manifest.c (search for sizeBegin
# inside DEBUG_MEM).
unreadVariable:src/info/manifest/manifest.c:247
